title: Diff heralds
pid: a56e42e2-b547-471c-96cd-f4fc99e0138b
body: |2-
  In the diff view (see the glossary's "Terms a user meets in the
  diff view"), the heralds show the three kinds of change: a vognode's =∃+=
  or =∃-= says that HEAD lacks it or the worktree does (its node's
  existence), its =M= ("modified") that both have it and its file
  records a change (its other properties), and =~+= or =~-= that HEAD
  or the worktree lacks its relationship to its viewparent. =M= stands
  where =∃= stands: a node is new, deleted or modified, never two of
  those. A folder member shows them as a content member does, so a
  modified write-protected member reads =☮ M=; its =~+= or =~-= is its
  relationship, of the folder's kind, to the folder's viewparent. On an alias, ID or
  flag member, which has one diff object, the same change facts read =+= and
  =-=, and the one object of an unknown viewnode or a restricted viewnode reads =~+= or =~-=. A =✓= after a diff object's change facts says its
  changes are marked processed, so =∃+✓ ~+= is a new node, approved,
  in a new relationship that is not; =M✓= is a modified node whose
  changes that neither its =~= nor a headline under it shows are marked
  (what =node= names there, 'docs/diff-view.org', "What a request names"). A processed aggregate (a
  folder or changeset marker) shows a =✓= when everything under it is processed. =request:processed= and
  =request:unprocessed=, with =:node=, =:rel=, =:newContent= or =:newOutward=, are pending processed
  requests, consumed by the next save.

  These heralds render the headline's one =(diff ...)= form (see
  'docs/api-and-formats.org', "Metadata of the diff view"): =∃+= and =∃-=
  come from its =(node added)= and =(node removed)= parts, =M= from
  =(properties changed)=, =~+= and =~-= from =(rel added)= and =(rel
  removed)= (the style keys 'existence' and 'properties' in
  'shared/herald-styles.json'), each =✓= from its =(processed ...)= part
  (=M='s, like =∃='s, from =node= there), and the pending processed request from the
  =(processed ...)= or =(unprocessed ...)= in the headline's =(editRequest ...)=;
  its =(leaves ...)= part shows nothing. The rules match the processed part once
  per diff object, so a =✓= follows its own object whatever the raw
  order of the parts.

  A ghost (a node the worktree lacks, shown with HEAD's
  text) reads =ᗣ☮=. The change viewnodes the view adds are labels: =old title
  and body= (or =old title=, =old body=) with its =body diff=, =⌂ -OLD
  +NEW= for a home skgrepo move, =relationship moved from OLD to NEW=, the
  changeset markers =its content changed= and =some content relationships
  changed skgrepo=, and a node's other changes, each in words, joined by
  "; " (=its content was reordered; its attribution changed=). A folder shown because its
  membership changed reads =its X changed (this file)= or =(other files)=
  (the forks folder, of both sides, =its forks changed=), with =✓= when every changed membership is processed; the server writes the
  folder as its bare atom, and the client sees the change in its
  members and draws that label, from 'folder_heralds' in
  'shared/herald-styles.json', in place of the folder's own. The
  nodeMerge folders read =it was acquired by= and =it acquired=.

  In the skgrepo-changes buffer a moved node's own heralds add =-OLD
  +NEW=, and ~Δ⌂=a~ when the nearest moved ancestor (here the parent, a)
  moved the same way.
